<TopicPage slug="subjects/music-technology/foundational/recording-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Recording is the art of converting a performance into a signal — cleanly, at the right level,
    with every choice made deliberately. It helps to picture the studio as a chain of
    translators: a voice or drum makes pressure waves in air; a microphone turns those waves into
    a tiny electrical voltage; a preamp raises that voltage to a workable level; a converter
    turns the voltage into numbers a computer can store. At every link, two enemies wait: noise
    rising from below and distortion dropping from above. Recording fundamentals is the craft of
    getting the music between them — every time, on every take, without panic.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does this matter so much? Because a recording is made <em>once</em> and mixed many times.
    Editing, tuning and mixing are powerful, but they only reshape what exists: digital tools can
    fix timing and pitch, yet a badly recorded source is a permanent tax — a vocal clipped at the
    converter cannot be un-clipped, a roomy thin guitar sound cannot lose its room. The
    discipline's oldest maxim is "capture, don't rescue": decisions made before pressing record
    are worth more than any plugin applied after.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fundamentals divide into three connected skills: choosing and placing microphones (which
    translator do you use, and where do you point it?), signal flow and gain staging (how loud is
    the signal at each link?), and capturing a performance (the human side — takes, monitoring,
    and the room itself). Everything on this page serves one test: does the raw recording sound
    like the source, at a healthy level, with no noise or clipping baked in?
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the signal chain and its two enemies" widget="equationStep" config={signalCfg} />

  <h2>Microphones and placement</h2>
  <p>
    Microphones fall into two great families. <strong>Dynamic</strong> microphones (the Shure
    SM57/SM58 family is the classic) work like a tiny loudspeaker in reverse: sound moves a
    diaphragm attached to a coil in a magnetic field, generating voltage. They are rugged,
    cheap-ish, tolerant of brutal sound-pressure levels and awkward rooms — which is why they
    live on guitar amplifiers, snare drums and stages. <strong>Condenser</strong> microphones
    instead measure the distance between a charged diaphragm and a backplate, giving them far
    finer detail and sensitivity — the large-diaphragm condenser is the default studio vocal
    mic — but they need +48 V <em>phantom power</em> down the cable, they hear everything
    (including bad rooms and computer fans), and they overload if you park one in front of a
    kick drum.
  </p>
  <p>
    Placement is the cheaper and mightier tool, and it works through three levers.
    <strong>Distance</strong> sets the direct-to-room blend: a vocal mic 2–5 cm from the lips is
    almost all direct sound, intimate and dry; pulled back to 30 cm it breathes with the room.
    <strong>Axis</strong> sets tone: most mics are brightest on-axis, so singing slightly
    off-axis tames harshness without EQ. <strong>The 3-to-1 rule</strong> governs multi-miking:
    when two mics capture the same source, keep their spacing at least three times the
    distance to the source, or the delay between them comb-filters the blend. Add
    <strong>proximity effect</strong> — directional mics boost bass as they get closer, which
    makes thin voices warm and boomy voices worse — and you can see why experienced engineers
    change the mic's position before reaching for EQ.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked case: recording a guitar amplifier. The standard move is a dynamic (SM57) a
    hand-width off the grille, aimed where the cone meets the dust cap — bright and present.
    Slide it towards the centre and the tone hardens; towards the edge of the cone and it
    darkens; pull it back a foot and the room arrives. The engineer's routine is to move the
    mic through those positions while the player repeats a loop, listening for the one where
    the tone is right at the source — because at the source, correction is free.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> expensive microphones make good recordings. A well-placed
    SM57 in a good position beats a boutique condenser badly placed every time; placement and
    the room dominate price in almost every practical test.
  </p>

  <h2>Signal flow and gain staging</h2>
  <p>
    Signal flow is the map of a studio: source → microphone → preamp → converter →
    recorder, and back out through playback. Gain staging is the discipline of setting each
    stage's level so the signal stays far above the noise floor and far below the clipping
    ceiling at every link. Think of it like water pressure through a series of pipes: each
    stage can be turned up, but the whole chain only works if the level is right at each joint.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two bookend failures are easy to state. <strong>Noise</strong>: if the signal is too
    quiet, the preamp's own hiss and the room's hum sit audibly underneath it, and no amount of
    later volume-raising separates them — you amplify the noise with the music.
    <strong>Clipping</strong>: if the signal overdrives a stage — especially the digital
    converter, which has an absolute ceiling (0 dBFS) — the waveform's tips are sliced off,
    and the harsh distortion is written permanently into the file. Digital recording
    concentrates the anxiety: there is no tape compression forgiving hot levels.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="signal, noise and headroom down the chain" widget="sliderLab" config={gainCfg} />

  <p>
    Run the numbers. A singer produces a source level around 60 dB at the mic; with 30 dB of
    preamp gain the signal lands at 90 dB against a 15 dB noise floor — about 75 dB of
    signal-to-noise, comfortable — with roughly 10 dB of headroom before the ceiling. Now raise
    the preamp to 45 dB: signal-to-noise improves to 90 dB, but headroom shrinks to under 5 dB,
    and one passionate chorus clips the take. The professional habit is to set gain during a
    confident rehearsal pass at performance level, then leave roughly 12–18 dB of headroom —
    quiet, healthy digital files cost nothing to lift later, but clipped ones cost everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> record as hot as possible like the tape era taught. Digital
    has no sweet spot at the top — 0 dBFS is a cliff, not a colour. Modern practice records at
    conservative levels and adds character in the mix on purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>Capturing a performance</h2>
  <p>
    The room is a band member. Every recording includes the space it happened in: reflections
    arrive milliseconds after the direct sound and define its character. Bedrooms are famously
    boxy because small rooms stack early reflections into comb filtering — a hollow, phasey
    sound that follows the take forever. Treated rooms and close-miking tame them; duvets,
    mattresses and closets are the honest budget versions. You can add artificial room later
    with reverb, but removing a bad room is one of audio's impossible tasks — which is why the
    discipline teaches clapping in the space and listening before pressing record.
  </p>
  <p>
    Capturing also means protecting the <em>performance</em>. Recording is a psychological
    situation: the engineer's first jobs are to make the player comfortable, to monitor
    (headphones) well enough that the artist can hear themselves, and to manage takes without
    breaking concentration. Practical protocol: dial in sounds before the artist arrives;
    record whole passes rather than fragments so musical flow survives; mark keeper takes
    live; comp (assemble) the best moments afterwards rather than punching in obsessively.
    Many great records were made on modest equipment with excellent performances — the reverse
    almost never happens.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the mix can fix anything. Mixing reshapes balance, space and
    tone within what was captured; it cannot invent a take's energy, restore clipped
    transients, or remove a room it was baked into. The recording IS the production.
  </p>

  <h2>Monitoring and the control room</h2>
  <p>
    Recording decisions are only as good as the monitoring they are made on: every choice —
    mic position, gain, the take itself — is judged through speakers or headphones, so the
    playback chain is as fundamental as the capture chain. <strong>Studio monitors</strong>
    (nearfield speakers placed at listening height, forming a triangle with the engineer's
    ears) are designed for flat, honest response — not to flatter the sound but to reveal
    its problems. <strong>Headphones</strong> complement them for detail work and for
    isolating what the artist needs to hear, at the cost of a slightly distorted sense of
    space. The practical literacy is <em>reference</em>: professionals play known, loved
    recordings through the same system to calibrate their ears before judging new material,
    because ears adapt to whatever they have been hearing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The room shapes monitoring the way it shapes capture. Speakers interact with surfaces:
    reflections smear stereo imaging, bass builds in corners and cancels at boundaries,
    and an untreated bedroom "mixes" a bass-heavy fiction that collapses on other systems.
    Budget studios fight back with the same physics in reverse — first-reflection panels,
    bass traps, the equilateral-triangle setup, and the humbling habit of checking mixes on
    earbuds, a laptop, and a car before calling them done. Capture and monitoring are one
    loop: you record through microphones, decide through speakers, and the discipline is
    keeping both links of the chain honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Polar patterns</strong> deserve study beyond "directional or not". Cardioid (the
    default) rejects the rear, ideal for isolating a vocal from a room; figure-8 hears front
    and back but rejects the sides — lovely for two singers face-to-face, and the basis of
    mid-side stereo technique; omni hears everything with no proximity effect and the most
    natural low end, and rewards good rooms. Pattern choice is a room strategy as much as a
    timbre choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The conversion stage</strong> has its own fundamentals: sample rate and bit depth.
    CD-standard 44.1 kHz/16-bit captures the audible band, but 24-bit recording buys around
    144 dB of theoretical dynamic range versus 96 dB — which is what lets modern engineers
    record 18 dB below the ceiling without noise anxiety. Higher sample rates (96 kHz) matter
    mostly for processing headroom and pitch-shifting quality, not audibility. <strong>Phantom
    power</strong> is worth respecting: +48 V down the mic line energises condensers, and
    plugging unbalanced or damaged gear into a phantom-fed input can damage it — a classic
    first-week fault.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, <strong>stereo and ensemble techniques</strong> extend the fundamentals: spaced
    pairs obey the 3-to-1 rule, coincident X/Y pairs trade width for mono compatibility, and
    drums become a system question (how many mics, how much bleed between them). Every one of
    these is still the same three skills in fancier clothes — right microphone, right
    placement, right level — and engineers who internalise that can walk into any room, with
    any kit, and get a clean recording of what actually happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Session documentation and file discipline</strong> are the fundamentals' quiet
    cousins, and studios run on them. A professional session logs its own anatomy: track
    names that match sources ("Kick-In", "GTR-L-57" rather than Audio-01), sample rate and
    session tempo recorded, mic and preamp settings noted so a recall is possible weeks
    later, and takes marked as they happen. Backups follow the versioned-copy habit — the
    session duplicated with a dated suffix at every significant milestone, one copy off the
    machine — because no mix has ever been saved by a corrupted hard drive. The reasoning
    is the same as the gain staging: the recording exists to be used later, by people
    (including future-you) who were not there. Notes that take two minutes per song save
    hours of guessing per album, and the engineer's reputation for recallable, well-named,
    backed-up sessions is worth as much as their ears.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Edexcel A Level Music Technology specification; Huber &amp; Runstein, <em>Modern Recording Techniques</em>; Sound on Sound recording guides.</p>
</TopicPage>
